Controller and DOM observations now drain in a common acquisition
order while retaining their independent bounded queues. A controller
release observed on blur must not overtake a preceding keyboard
press mapped to the same action. That would falsely release and
re-press the shared action. The browser adapter assigns an order to
each controller observation batch and each DOM transition, independent
of coarse browser clock timestamps. A DOM overflow replaces its own
queue only; preceding controller edges and subsequent controller
cancellation retain their order. The inline browser fixture verifies
the shared router emits only the final release on focus loss, and
that an actual blur still releases the controller when the overflowed
DOM queue cannot admit the blur observation.

